  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Opened on 1 July 2026 and inaugurated on 30 July by the mayor, the Pipady dog beach is Toulon's second swimming area dedicated to dogs, after the Tabarly cove. It is set up in an inlet to the right of the Tour Royale, in former moats. The 2,000 sqm space is fully fenced, letting dogs roam completely freely, separated from the site's other users (toulon.fr). The city specifies that it welcomes animals of all sizes, and that the beach is gravel or fine gravel.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the city of Toulon, in its dedicated areas dogs can swim or run off-leash and freely, under their owner's supervision. Two-way signage organises circulation: swimmers and visitors are asked to head left, while dogs have their own lane to the right. Owners must pick up droppings, and bag dispensers are installed nearby. Fishing is now banned in the area, to avoid injuries from hooks.</p>
      <p>Before opening, the city had the water quality analysed, which confirmed the absence of cyanobacteria in the dog swimming area. The mayor announced a water point "soon" on 30 July 2026: in the meantime, bring water for your dog. Local residents describe the spot as shaded in the morning, and the town hall reminds visitors that the beach is open every day.</p>
      <p>To get there, parking is free at the Tour Royale car park, and the beach can be reached on foot from Le Mourillon; the city is working with the Mistral network to improve bus service. The neighbouring Pipady beach has a shower, which may be subject to usage restrictions, and a lifeguard post. On the rest of the Mourillon beaches and lawns, dogs remain banned, even on a leash.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: if your dog is not a strong swimmer, this sheltered inlet is, according to owners interviewed at the inauguration, more reassuring than the Tabarly cove; come in the morning, when the spot is in the shade.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Tour Royale, a listed historic monument, overlooks the dog beach; the project was led with the architect of the Bâtiments de France and no equipment is fixed on the "big tower". Toulon's other dog beach, the <a href="/en/fiche-anse-tabarly-toulon">Tabarly cove</a>, is in the Mourillon area; the city says it is destined to disappear with the redevelopment works on the nautical base, with Pipady due to replace it eventually. Toulon also has 30 dog toilet sites (canisites), including 15 enclosed dog parks of more than 100 sqm.</p>
